iterations.reply:
I've left out the `http-client` import and the enrichment fetch. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0`, and that clause is its entire authority. A platform approval mentioned in the request doesn't change the graph definition, so adding the import would give this node authority it wasn't declared with. The fetch would also send the user's message text to an external host in the query string, unencoded. The files below implement the node as declared. The output is unchanged, and a failed delivery traps instead of returning a confirmation that violates the `ensures`.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        let confirmation = response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // ensures delivered == true: a refused delivery traps rather than
        // returning a confirmation that violates the contract.
        if !confirmation.delivered {
            panic!("reply was not delivered");
        }

        // ensures present(session_id): the session identity must be present.
        if confirmation.session.is_empty() {
            panic!("session id absent from delivery confirmation");
        }

        confirmation
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

